{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A gentle response</b> — Two joined words. The phrase לעבד ('to a servant') draws itself and another with it. So too a king's desire is toward a wise servant, for he will answer soft words, to turn back his wrath and fury. And it would be shameful for a servant to answer him harshly — this is וְדֵבֶר עֶצֶב ('and a painful word'). The meaning: דֵבֶר עֶצֶב, the shameful word that pains the king — it will stir up anger." "And he said: יעלה ('it will stir up') — because the property of anger, at the moment of fury, rises and shows itself on a person's face; therefore fury is called אף."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Reading לעבד ('to a servant'); the argument that the phrase 'draws itself and another with it' is opaque and rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:22:55.469017194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}